> There are solutions, don't get me wrong.  Just none of them are as
> straightforward as one would think.

Right.  I'm really just tossing out random ideas here, both because
there's always the off chance that one of them might actually be
useful and because it's an interesting problem to consider.

The real issue, of course, is that shopkeepers are supposed to be
acting as if they're not afraid of the player, when the player has
every motivation to do them in and take their stuff.  That's a tricky
one to solve in any case -- having the shopkeepers come to the door
just brings it a little more to the foreground (sorry).

Some possible solutions include:

 * Making shopkeepers tougher.  They already are, but they could be a
   little more so yet.  In particular, making them faster could help.

 * Making the other consequences of attacking and/or killing
   shopkeepers worse.  Summoning the Kops could be one possibility,
   but if you can kill shopkeepers with impunity, the Kops aren't
   going to be much of an obstacle either.  Maybe killing one
   shopkeeper could anger them all.  Other than those, I can't really
   think of anything off the top of my head, short of arbitrary divine
   punishment.

 * Making live shopkeepers more useful.  Right now, their main utility
   is price-ID'ing, although they do have limited tactical uses as
   well (one can hide in a shop with an unpaid item in inventory).
   This is generally enough to persuade me not to kill them even when
   I could, but it may not be enough for everybody.  Adding the repair
   and charging services from Slash'Em would go a long way in this
   direction, but it would certainly have major game balance effects
   as well.


>> Alternatively, for a very cheap-and-easy solution, make shop doors
>> magically reflective (or beam-absorbing) even when open.
>
